<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:app="using:Mm2Lobby.App"
        x:Class="Mm2Lobby.App.MainWindow"
        x:Name="Root"
        x:DataType="app:LobbyViewModel"
        Title="MM2 Lobby" Width="880" Height="830"
        MinWidth="880" MinHeight="700"
        SizeChanged="OnWindowSizeChanged"
        Closing="OnWindowClosing">

  <!-- The worst case for the layout is the Setup panel open with four rows
       that report a problem, the message of the network ID on screen, and the
       message of the last fix on screen. A probe measured that case on macOS
       at 880 wide, and these are the heights:

         the two editors            108 (72 without the message of the ID)
         the Setup panel            421 (its rows 308, its header, its
                                    padding and the Check again button 113)
         the message of the fix      70
         the host panel and buttons  62
         the margins                 52

       So every row except the Setup panel and the list of peers takes 320.

       MinHeight is 700. The work area of a 1366 by 768 screen is about 728
       with a taskbar, and Windows refuses to shrink a window below its
       minimum. A minimum above the work area therefore puts the two buttons
       under the taskbar, where the player cannot reach them.

       The Setup panel yields the height, not the list and not the buttons.
       OnWindowSizeChanged caps the box that holds the rows, so the panel
       scrolls on a short window. At 830 the cap is 340, the rows need 308,
       and no scrollbar appears. At 700 the cap is 210, the rows scroll, the
       list keeps 57, and the buttons end 32 above the bottom edge.

       These numbers come from macOS. Windows draws the text at another size,
       so they move there. The cap gives the list away before it clips the
       buttons, which is the right order.

       The title of the host panel takes one line while the app races. It
       takes three lines while the app cannot reach ZeroTier, because it then
       names the state and the one action that repairs it. The list of peers
       gives that height, as it gives the height of the report below. I
       measured no new number on macOS for this piece, and the three buttons
       keep the order of the cap: the list yields before they do.

       The report of the last start adds about 36 to the host panel. That
       panel appears after the player presses the launch button, and MM2 then
       covers the screen. The list of peers gives that height, because the
       list holds the star row. The cap of the Setup panel does not move,
       because that panel is shut by then: every check passed, or the player
       read the rows and pressed the button all the same. -->

  <Grid Margin="16" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,*,Auto">

    <!-- The two editors of the configuration. The window owns the one
         AppConfig, and these two boxes are the only place where the player
         changes it. One Grid holds both rows, so the two boxes line up. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*"
          RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,0,0,12">

      <!-- The name editor. The limit comes from PeerTable. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Your name" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,8,0" />
      <TextBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="NameBox" Width="260" Margin="0,0,8,0"
               MaxLength="{Binding DisplayNameMaxLength}"
               Text="{Binding DisplayName, Mode=TwoWay,
                              UpdateSourceTrigger=LostFocus}" />
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" VerticalAlignment="Center" Foreground="Gray"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 Text="Click outside the box. The others then see the new name after two seconds." />

      <!-- The network ID editor. Without it, a fresh install has no way to
           reach a lobby: the ZeroTier check fails, and the app blocks Host
           and Join.

           This box has no MaxLength. A player pastes the ID, a paste can
           carry a space at each end, and a limit of 16 would cut two
           characters off such a paste. NetworkIdRule removes the spaces. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Text="ZeroTier network ID"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,8,8,0" />
      <TextBox Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="NetworkIdBox" Width="260"
               Margin="0,8,8,0"
               Text="{Binding ZeroTierNetworkId, Mode=TwoWay,
                              UpdateSourceTrigger=LostFocus}" />
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2" VerticalAlignment="Center" Foreground="Gray"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,8,0,0"
                 Text="Click outside the box. The app then saves the ID." />

      <!-- What the last write of the ID did. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                 Text="{Binding NetworkIdMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 IsVisible="{Binding HasNetworkIdMessage}" FontWeight="Bold"
                 Margin="0,8,0,0" />
    </Grid>

    <!-- The setup list. It opens while a check fails. -->
    <Expander Grid.Row="1" Header="Setup" IsExpanded="{Binding SetupExpanded}"
              HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" Margin="0,0,0,12">
      <StackPanel Spacing="8">

        <!-- The rows scroll, and the Check again button below stays outside
             this box. A short window must not put that button out of reach.
             OnWindowSizeChanged sets MaxHeight from the height of the window.
             The value here covers the first layout only. -->
        <ScrollViewer x:Name="SetupScroll" MaxHeight="340"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding CheckRows}">
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="app:CheckRow">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,90,*,Auto" Margin="0,6">
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Name}" FontWeight="Bold"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Mark}" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Spacing="2">
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Message}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Advice}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Foreground="Gray" />
                </StackPanel>
                <Button Grid.Column="3" Content="{Binding ActionLabel}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding ShowAction}" Tag="{Binding Name}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding #Root.((app:LobbyViewModel)DataContext).CanAct}"
                        Click="OnFixClick" Margin="8,0,0,0" Padding="12,4"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top" />
              </Grid>
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <Button Content="Check again" Click="OnCheckAgainClick"
                IsEnabled="{Binding CanAct}"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left" Padding="12,4" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Expander>

    <!-- What the last fix reported. This sits outside the Expander above,
         because that panel closes itself once every check passes, and the
         firewall Repair runs only while they all do. -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Text="{Binding FixMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               IsVisible="{Binding HasFixMessage}" FontWeight="Bold"
               Margin="0,0,0,12" />

    <!-- The lobby list. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="3" RowDefinitions="Auto,*">
      <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="*,200,130,80,190" Margin="4,0,4,4">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Player" FontWeight="Bold" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="State" FontWeight="Bold" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="Address" FontWeight="Bold" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" Text="Link" FontWeight="Bold" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Text="Warning" FontWeight="Bold" />
      </Grid>

      <Border Grid.Row="1" BorderBrush="Gray" BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="4">
        <ScrollViewer>
          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding PeerRows}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate x:DataType="app:PeerRow">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,200,130,80,190" Margin="4,3">
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Name}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding State}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding Address}" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" Text="{Binding Link}" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Text="{Binding VersionNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             IsVisible="{Binding VersionDiffers}" FontWeight="Bold" />
                </Grid>
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>
        </ScrollViewer>
      </Border>
    </Grid>

    <!-- The host panel and the two buttons. -->
    <StackPanel Grid.Row="4" Spacing="4" Margin="0,12,0,0">
      <!-- This line carries the one sentence of a race, and it also carries
           the three sentences of a state that the app cannot race in. So it
           wraps. -->
      <TextBlock Text="{Binding HostTitle}" FontWeight="Bold" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      <SelectableTextBlock Text="{Binding HostAddress}" FontSize="30" FontWeight="Bold"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasHostAddress}" />

      <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding JoinSteps}" Margin="0,0,0,4">
        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          <DataTemplate x:DataType="x:String">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,1" />
          </DataTemplate>
        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
      </ItemsControl>

      <TextBlock Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 IsVisible="{Binding HasStatusMessage}" Foreground="Gray" />
      <TextBlock Text="{Binding FaultMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 IsVisible="{Binding HasFaultMessage}" FontWeight="Bold" />

      <!-- The report of the last start through the DirectPlay lobby. The step
           of a failed start asks the player to send a report to the person who
           runs the group, and only this text holds the answer of each
           DirectPlay call. The panel appears after the player presses the
           launch button, and it is shut, so it costs the window 32 and not the
           height of the text. -->
      <Expander Header="The report of the last start" IsExpanded="False"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasLaunchReport}"
                HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" Margin="0,4,0,0">
        <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="160" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
          <SelectableTextBlock Text="{Binding LaunchReport}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </ScrollViewer>
      </Expander>

      <!-- Host and Join announce the player, and on the lobby path each of
           them also starts the game in the race. The third button starts MM2.
           Every label comes from the view model, because each one names what
           the press will do: a label that promises a race on a path that
           shows an address alone is a lie in a button.

           On the fallback path Host and Join start no game. MM2 covers the
           whole screen, so the address above must reach the player first, and
           the third button then starts the game. After any start, the third
           button offers a second start, which the owner needs when MM2
           stops. -->
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" Margin="0,8,0,0">
        <Button Content="{Binding HostButtonText}" IsEnabled="{Binding CanHost}"
                Click="OnHostClick" Padding="14,6" />
        <Button Content="{Binding JoinButtonText}" IsEnabled="{Binding CanJoin}"
                Click="OnJoinClick" Padding="14,6" />
        <Button Content="{Binding StartGameButtonText}" IsEnabled="{Binding CanStartGame}"
                Click="OnStartGameClick" Padding="14,6" />
      </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>

  </Grid>
</Window>
